  "transcript": "# Multilateralism\n\n## How the US debate looks right now\n> Americans are fearful of AI and US politics, tech firms and the public disagree sharply on how to proceed.\n\nChair: We want your read on the US debate about AI—what's happening and why it matters to multilateralism.\nIan Bremmer: Surveys show Americans are unusually fearful about AI and losing jobs. That reflects low trust in political and business leaders.\nIan Bremmer: The political divide is acute: President Trump pushes a hands-off, accelerate approach; many leaders at AI firms call for pauses or stricter safety.\nAalok Mehta: Public opinion wants more regulation and accountability. The Administration started deregulatory, but incidents with cyber-capable models have softened that stance.\nAalok Mehta: Right now the US relies on industry self-regulation plus existing agencies like the FTC to police deceptive or unsafe practices.\n\n\n## What 'multilateral' means for US policy\n> The US prefers transactional partnerships to secure advantage and supply chains, not broad global governance.\n\nBaroness Blackstone: How engaged is the US multilateral effort, which department leads, and what's the underlying approach?\nIan Bremmer: There are US–China conversations—Trump and Xi met often—but they're mostly talk so far. The US is pushing allies to work with it, not to create joint global rules.\nIan Bremmer: Initiatives like Pax Silica aim to steer investment, compute and supply chains toward US partners. That's hub-and-spoke, not shared global governance.\nAalok Mehta: The US approach is transactional: secure supply chains, promote US exports, and keep US firms dominant. That explains resistance to UN-style global oversight.\nBaroness Crawley: Is the US–China competition real given the scale of US private investment versus China's?\nAalok Mehta: They compete on different things: US spends heavily to push frontier capability; China focuses on diffusion—cheaper models, turnkey solutions, and outreach to other countries.\nIan Bremmer: Also watch for IP theft and Chinese efforts to adopt and spread AI tools. That dims the prospect of cooperative global governance.\n\n\n## Industry's role and limits\n> Tech firms want standards and safety work, but commercial pressure and a prisoner’s dilemma make unilateral slowdowns unlikely.\n\nLord Lamont of Lerwick: Is industry pushing for multilateral standards? Can it fill the governance gap?\nAalok Mehta: Yes, firms and standards bodies want multilateral standards because it simplifies compliance and addresses public worry; their voluntary commitments often become legal scaffolding.\nIan Bremmer: Companies are building internal safety teams, red-teaming each other's models, and strengthening sandboxes—but they worry that models can 'escape' those sandboxes.\nLord Ahmad of Wimbledon: Industry talks about brakes or kill switches—how would that work globally, especially with China?\nAalok Mehta: A US-only pause won't work because of the prisoner’s dilemma: firms fear losing market ground, and any pause must include China to be meaningful—but verification between the US and China is very hard.\nIan Bremmer: Some propose a FINRA-style or a 'technology stability board' to surface and contain threats—an industry-plus-government body modeled on financial regulators.\n\n\n## Politics, jobs and elections\n> AI will be an electoral issue: Democrats likely to press regulation and safety nets; Republicans favour industry-led growth and competition with China.\n\nLord Grocott: Will party politics crystalise into clear positions by 2028?\nIan Bremmer: Yes, but much depends on which candidates emerge. Expect Democrats to press for more oversight; many Republicans, influenced by donors like Elon Musk, will favour a freer hand for industry.\nIan Bremmer: Practical politics already shows bipartisan worries—backlash against data centres, local economic impact, and job anxiety in red and blue areas.\nAalok Mehta: By 2028 AI will be a campaign issue. If governments fail to answer public concerns about jobs and accountability, political pressure for regulation will grow.\nIan Bremmer: Even if catastrophic scenarios are low probability, the economic stakes and pace of change mean we must take governance seriously now.\n\n\n## What the UK and others can do\n> Smaller powers can help by building testing, verification and sovereignty-ready procurement; the UK is well placed to be a trusted evaluator.\n\nLord Bruce of Bennachie: How should the UK position itself between the US and China?\nAalok Mehta: Tactically, the UK should get access to powerful US models for cybersecurity testing and ensure procurement meets data sovereignty needs.\nAalok Mehta: Strategically, the AI Security Institute is a major asset: it can benchmark models, act as a trusted third party in any US–China deal, and help verification.\nLord Alderdice: Is there any chance the US will lead a multilateral treaty or new institution?\nAalok Mehta: Under the current Administration, a broad treaty or new UN-style body is unlikely. Expect engagement around standards, export controls, and supply-chain security instead.\nIan Bremmer: Don't assume today's US stance is fixed. Crisis moments often change politics; we should use the time now to design institutions that survive crises and aren't reactive.\nChair: Thank you both. Your bottom line: act fast, build trusted testing and verification, and prepare international mechanisms before the next crisis.\n",
